{
  "title": "CHAPTER 5: BANKING IN INDIA",
  "order": 3,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "n7el8o6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO BANKING"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) regulates the banking system under the Banking Regulation Act, 1949.",
        "Bank nationalisation occurred in two major phases: 14 banks in 1969 and 6 banks in 1980.",
        "A loan asset becomes a Non-Performing Asset (NPA) when interest or principal remains overdue for over 90 days.",
        "Domestic commercial banks are mandated to allocate 40% of Adjusted Net Bank Credit (ANBC) to Priority Sector Lending.",
        "Marginal Cost of Funds Based Lending Rate (MCLR) was introduced in 2016, replacing the Base Rate system to improve monetary policy transmission."
      ],
      "id": "jy8er09"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
